fastened with a golden cord having a jewel at the center knot;

In place of both mother and father, a wise man should remember the guru alone;
what is not obtained through the guru is indeed not found anywhere else.
Through the guru's grace one attains everything; of this there is no doubt.
Medhāvī, Kapila, and Sumati of great austerity,
attained immortality through the proper service of their guru Gautama. / Therefore, with all effort, devoted to Viṣṇu in Kārtika,
one should perform service to the guru; therefrom one will attain liberation.
The best among twice-born who bestows Vaiṣṇava duty upon men,
attains the merit of giving the earth along with the oceans.
A cow fashioned of sesame, gold, silver, land, and clothing—
they shall make gifts of cows with wholehearted devotion, O you of good vows.
The gift of a maiden is superior to all gifts. / A thousand cows is equal to a hundred oxen;
a carriage is equal to ten oxen; a horse is equal to ten carriages;
the gift of an elephant is superior to thousands of gifts of horses;
and the gift of gold is equal to thousands of gifts of elephants;

One should wave the apāmārga and prapunnāḍa over one's head.
Having bathed, with wet clothes, one should offer a lamp to the two sons of Mṛtyu:
the two dogs, Śyāma and Śabala, brothers and servants of Yama;
the two sons of Mṛtyu are satisfied on the fourteenth by the gift of a lamp.
One should perform this ritual ablution together with loved ones and relatives;
having performed the water-offering belonging to the bath, one should then make a water-offering to Yama:
'To Yama, to the King of Dharma, to Death, and to the Ender;
to the son of Vivasvat, to Time, and to the Destroyer of all beings;
to Audumbara, to Dadhna, to the Dark, to the Supreme Lord;
to Vṛkodara, to Citra, to Citragupta—homage to you!'
These should be the fourteen mantras, each accompanied by 'namas';
with each one, one should offer three palmfuls of water mixed with sesame seeds.
It may be performed with the sacred thread over the left shoulder or over the right shoulder;
for Yama possesses a two-fold nature: both godhead and ancestorhood.
Even one whose father is living should perform the water-offering to Yama and Bhīṣma;

his mind astonished, to see the immoral ways in the Kali age.
There he saw dreadful people, wicked and intent on sinful conduct:
all the twice-born were heretics, and śūdras were wandering ascetics;
the wife hates her husband, and the disciple hates his teacher;
the servant kills his master, and the son is intent on murdering his father;
all the twice-born are practically śūdras, and cows are like goats;
the Vedas are reduced to mere popular songs, and sacred rites are treated as ordinary actions;
ghosts, spirits, goblins and the like are the deities granting fruits there,
and these alone do people, devoted to sin and sharp [in deceit], worship with faith;
all are devoted to sexual intercourse, staking their lives for its sake;
giving false testimony, with minds always deceitful;
one thing is in the mind, another in speech, and another in deed, always in the Kali age;
the learning of all is rationalistic debate, and that is honoured in the royal palace;
the arts and knowledges such as singing bring delight to kings;
the low attain honour, and not the noble, in the Kali age;

all the twice-born learned in the Veda become impoverished in the Kali age;
devotion to Viṣṇu scarcely exists among men;
sacred fields will mostly be full of heretics;
śūdras are expositors of dharma, and ascetics have matted locks in Kali;
all mortals are short-lived, devoid of compassion, and deceitful;
all are expositors of dharma, and all delight in receiving [gifts];
they desire worship for themselves, and are given to baseless slander;
all are given to envy when their master arrives at his own house;
a brother violates his sister, and a father indeed his daughter, in Kali;
all are devoted to śūdra women, and all consort with courtesans;
they do not recognize the virtuous, and regard them as great sinners;
in their stubborn malice they expose a single fault of the good;
they speak of the collection of faults of the sinful as virtues;
the devoid of virtues in Kali grasp only at faults;
a leech possessing its nature drinks blood, not milk;

medicinal herbs are destitute of potency, and the seasons are inverted;
famine reigns in all kingdoms; a maiden does not give birth at the proper time;
men take pleasure in the arts of actors and dancers in Kali;
those devoted to the learning of the Vedas and the Upaniṣads, possessing superior virtues,
fools view as servants; they are completely fallen, O king;
all have abandoned funeral rites, and abandoned the rites prescribed by the Veda;
the names of Viṣṇu are never on their tongues;
immersed in erotic sentiment, they sing only its songs;
no service to Viṣṇu, no discourse on scriptures, no consecration for sacrifice, not the least reflection,
no pilgrimage to sacred fords, no virtues of giving—wonderfully, none existed anywhere among the people in Kali.
Seeing them, Dharmavarṇa was greatly frightened and utterly astonished.
Seeing his lineage going to destruction through sin, he went to another continent.
Wandering through all the continents and worlds everywhere,
the wise man once, moved by curiosity, went to the realm of the ancestors.

All kinds of food, both chewable and eatable, are exceedingly splendid;
yet through contact with it they turn into feces—how then can that body bring happiness?
Fragrant and cool water turns into urine through contact with it;
tell me now, O monkey: how could that lump [of flesh] be beautiful?
Indeed, garments that are exceedingly white and clean
become soiled with sweat through contact with it—how could it be beautiful?
Listen to the supreme truth from me, O Hanūmat, son of the Wind!
In this pit of saṃsāra, no happiness at all is to be found.
First a living creature meets with birth, and after that, childhood;
afterwards he attains youth, and then experiences old age;
thereafter he meets death, and then undergoes birth again.
It is solely through the power of ignorance that man experiences suffering;
upon the cessation of that ignorance, however, he attains supreme bliss.
The cessation of ignorance comes from knowledge alone, not from ritual action.
Knowledge is truly the supreme Brahman; knowledge is born of the statements of the Upaniṣads.

The best among recipients is one devoted to Vedic conduct;
even higher than him is the recipient who has no śūdra's food in his belly;
he whose regular practice consists continuously of the Vedas, the Purāṇa mantras,
the worship of Śiva, Viṣṇu, and others, and the observances of the classes and life-stages;
and who is poor and has a family—such a recipient is said to be the foremost.
What is given to such a recipient truly yields righteousness, pleasure, wealth, and liberation.
In a sacred place, a gift given to one who is not a true recipient is especially condemned;
otherwise, one will become a chameleon for ten births;
for three births he will be a donkey, and a frog for two births;
for one birth an outcaste (cāṇḍāla), and then he will become a śūdra;
and after that he is born in turn as a kṣatriya, a vaiśya, and a brahmin,
and there he will be poor and afflicted with many diseases.
Thus many kinds of faults arise from giving to an unworthy recipient;
therefore, by every effort, one should have gifts given to worthy recipients.
If such a recipient is not found, then, with a prior vow,
designating a single worthy recipient, one should pour water onto the ground.
Upon the death of the designated recipient, one should bestow it upon his son;
should his death occur as well, one should offer it to Mahādeva.
Therefore, one should never give to an unworthy recipient, especially at a sacred ford.
Śrī Sūta said:
Addressed in this manner by Vasiṣṭha, that Dilīpa, O best of brahmins,
from that time forward gave excellent gifts to worthy recipients.
Therefore, at this holy place, at the bridge, even here, O foremost of sages,
if a worthy recipient is found, then one should give wealth and the like.
If not, having made a vow, designating an eminent and worthy recipient,
one should pour water upon the earth with devotion.
Having returned to one's own village, one should afterwards offer it to that recipient.
Otherwise, the wealth previously resolved upon will cause a loss of merit.
He does not experience suffering again, but attains sāyujya liberation.

